<Base
  title="About — Journeyletter"
  description="What the Journeyletter is and the four rules it runs under."
>
  <h1 style="margin-top:2.5rem;">About</h1>
  <p>
    The Journeyletter is field notes on operating AI agents for real work, written for
    serious agent-builders. It documents one practitioner's stack — the delegation
    policies, the contracts, the struggles, the reversals — as they actually happen. It
    is published by <a href="/">Pezi Labs</a> and edited by Tom Murphy.
  </p>
  <p>The publication runs under four rules:</p>

  <h2>Exhaust, not authorship</h2>
  <p>
    Nobody sits down to "write a post." The work happens; an agent harvests the exhaust —
    repo history, decision records, session transcripts — and surfaces when there's a
    piece worth making. A publication that demands homework from its author dies.
  </p>

  <h2>Cadence by accumulation</h2>
  <p>
    It publishes when there's something real, not on a calendar. No filler, ever. If you
    don't hear from it, nothing worth your time happened.
  </p>

  <h2>The wall</h2>
  <p>
    Patterns, struggles, and learnings cross into these pieces. Personal data never does
    — health, family, and finances stay categorically behind the wall, filtered out at
    the harvest stage before a draft exists. Field notes, not oversharing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="colophons">Full transparency</h2>
  <p>
    Every piece ends with a colophon naming the stack, the agents that did the work, and
    exactly what the human did. The colophons are structured data, and over time they are
    the experiment: watch the human's role shrink from author to editor to mentor, piece
    by piece, with receipts.
  </p>
</Base>
